key|label|kind|level|lesson|collection|definition|example|imageBrief
motor-scooter|motor scooter|vehicle|2|land|road|A motor vehicle with a seat and a platform for the rider's feet.|The rider sits on the motor scooter.|Whole seated motor scooter; compare with the standing kick scooter, without a rider hiding its shape.
auto-rickshaw|auto-rickshaw|vehicle|3|land|road|A small motor vehicle with three wheels that can carry passengers.|Passengers sit behind the auto-rickshaw driver.|Three visible wheels and passenger cabin; distinct from pulled and cycle rickshaws.
cycle-rickshaw|cycle rickshaw|vehicle|3|land|human-powered|A passenger vehicle moved by a person pedalling.|The rider pedals the cycle rickshaw.|Complete pedal drive and passenger seat; no engine or pulling handles.
coach|coach|vehicle|2|land|passengers|A large passenger vehicle often used for longer road journeys.|Our group travels to another town by coach.|Whole coach with luggage compartment context; compare local bus without implying all buses are identical.
double-decker-bus|double-decker bus|vehicle|2|land|passengers|A bus with passenger seats on two levels.|The double-decker bus has an upper deck.|Two clear levels of windows and a small cutaway showing two passenger floors.
tow-truck|tow truck|vehicle|2|helping|work|A truck used to carry or pull a vehicle that needs help.|The tow truck carries a broken-down car.|Whole flatbed truck with a secured car; safe roadside context and no child nearby.
refuse-truck|refuse truck|vehicle|2|helping|work|A truck that collects and carries rubbish.|The refuse truck collects rubbish from our street.|Clear refuse body and bin lifting equipment at rest; workers away from moving machinery.
concrete-mixer|concrete mixer truck|vehicle|3|helping|work|A truck with a turning drum that carries and mixes concrete.|The concrete mixer truck brings concrete to the building site.|Whole truck with an unmistakable large mixing drum.
tipper-truck|tipper truck|vehicle|3|helping|work|A truck with a container that can lift to tip out a load.|The tipper truck unloads a pile of gravel.|Paired level and raised container views; no people in unloading area.
rowboat|rowboat|vehicle|2|water|human-powered|A small boat moved using oars.|A person uses oars to move the rowboat.|Whole boat and two oars clearly contacting water; lifejacket shown if occupied.
sailboat|sailboat|vehicle|2|water|water|A boat that can use wind in its sails to move.|Wind fills the sailboat's sail.|Whole hull, mast and sail; no inference that all yachts are sailboats.
canoe|canoe|vehicle|3|water|human-powered|A narrow boat often moved with a single-bladed paddle.|The paddler moves the canoe across calm water.|Open canoe and single-bladed paddle; compare kayak with visible differences.
kayak|kayak|vehicle|3|water|human-powered|A narrow boat often paddled with a blade at each end of the paddle.|The kayaker uses a double-bladed paddle.|Seated paddler and double-bladed paddle; appropriate fitted water-safety equipment.
tugboat|tugboat|vehicle|3|water|work|A strong boat that helps move other boats or ships.|The tugboat helps a ship near the harbour.|Tug beside a larger ship with safe visible relationship, plus whole-subject inset.
cargo-bicycle|cargo bicycle|vehicle|3|helping|human-powered|A bicycle built with space to carry a load.|The cargo bicycle carries parcels.|Complete pedals, wheels and load box; no hidden powered variant.
rocket|rocket|vehicle|2|air|space|A vehicle that can use rocket engines to carry a load upwards or into space.|The rocket lifts away from its launch pad.|Recognisable launch rocket and safe distant pad; separate from the winged shuttle orbiter.
wheel|wheel|part|1|land|vehicle-parts|A round part that turns to help a vehicle move.|The car's wheels turn as it moves.|One complete wheel highlighted on an intact car; distinguish wheel from tyre.
tyre|tyre|part|2|land|vehicle-parts|The outer covering around many vehicle wheels.|The bicycle tyre touches the ground.|Matched wheel and tyre comparison with tyre highlighted; no label-only distinction.
pedal|pedal|part|1|land|vehicle-parts|A part that you push with your foot to work a bicycle or another machine.|The cyclist pushes the bicycle pedals.|Bicycle pedal close-up linked to complete bicycle; show foot contact safely.
handlebars|handlebars|part|2|land|vehicle-parts|The bars a rider holds to steer a bicycle or similar vehicle.|The rider holds the handlebars with both hands.|Bicycle handlebars highlighted on a complete bicycle.
steering-wheel|steering wheel|part|2|land|vehicle-parts|A wheel-shaped control used to steer many vehicles.|The driver turns the steering wheel.|Clearly mounted steering wheel in a parked car; adult driver if shown.
brake|brake|part|2|land|vehicle-parts|A part that helps slow or stop a vehicle.|The bicycle brake helps slow the wheel.|Bicycle brake lever connected visually to brake at the wheel; no unexplained loose component.
engine|engine|part|2|helping|vehicle-parts|A machine that supplies power to move many vehicles.|The engine powers the truck.|Engine context in a safely parked truck with an explanatory cutaway.
seat|seat|part|1|everyday|vehicle-parts|A place made for a person to sit.|The passenger sits on a bus seat.|One bus seat highlighted in a complete cabin view.
seat-belt|seat belt|part|1|safety|safe-journeys|A strap that helps hold someone in their seat.|An adult checks the passenger's seat belt.|Adult checks restraint in parked vehicle; do not imply an adult belt fits every child.
door|door|part|1|everyday|vehicle-parts|A movable part that opens and closes an entrance.|The bus door opens at the stop.|Whole stationary bus and highlighted passenger door; safe pavement boarding side.
window|window|part|1|everyday|vehicle-parts|An opening with glass or another clear material that lets us see through.|We look through the train window.|Window highlighted within complete train carriage; seated passenger.
headlight|headlight|part|2|land|vehicle-parts|A light on the front of a vehicle that helps light the way.|The car's headlights shine ahead.|Matched front view with clear headlights, no eye-like face treatment.
indicator|indicator|part|3|land|vehicle-parts|A flashing vehicle light used to signal a planned turn.|The driver signals with an indicator before turning.|Matched sequence with highlighted lamp; do not teach that a signal guarantees a turn.
mirror|vehicle mirror|part|2|safety|vehicle-parts|A mirror that helps a driver or rider see around or behind a vehicle.|The driver checks the vehicle mirror.|Side mirror mounted on parked vehicle with believable reflection; no promise of full visibility.
wing|wing|part|1|air|vehicle-parts|A shaped part of an aircraft that helps support it in flight.|The airplane has a wing on each side.|Whole airplane and highlighted wing; no face or missing far wing.
rotor|rotor|part|3|air|vehicle-parts|A set of turning blades that helps a helicopter fly.|The helicopter's main rotor turns above its cabin.|Whole helicopter with rotor above body, plus still close-up; no unsafe approach.
propeller|propeller|part|3|air|vehicle-parts|Turning blades that push air or water to help move a vehicle.|The airplane's propeller turns at the front.|Propeller airplane with complete visible propeller; explain boat propellers separately.
sail|sail|part|2|water|vehicle-parts|A large sheet of strong material that catches wind to help move a boat.|Wind pushes against the sail.|Complete sailboat with sail highlighted; show mast and attachment context.
anchor|anchor|part|3|water|vehicle-parts|A heavy object lowered from a boat to help hold it in place.|The boat lowers its anchor.|Boat, line and anchor shown together in a simple water cutaway.
carriage|train carriage|part|2|land|rail|A connected section of a train that can carry passengers.|Passengers sit in the train carriage.|Passenger carriage connected behind locomotive; no road coach ambiguity.
road|road|place|1|land|journey-places|A way made for vehicles to travel along.|The bus travels along the road.|Short road scene with kerbs and pavement; safe separation of pedestrians.
pavement|pavement|place|1|safety|journey-places|A path beside a road for people to walk along.|We walk with an adult on the pavement.|Clear raised walking path beside road; local terminology alias sidewalk.
cycle-lane|cycle lane|place|2|land|journey-places|A marked part of a route intended for bicycles.|A cyclist follows the marked cycle lane.|Clear cycle marking and route boundaries; no claim every lane is physically protected.
pedestrian-crossing|pedestrian crossing|place|1|safety|safe-journeys|A place marked for people to cross a road.|We find a crossing with a trusted adult.|Adult and child waiting back from kerb; traffic stopped before crossing; local design review required.
junction|junction|place|2|land|journey-places|A place where roads meet.|The car slows near the road junction.|Simple two-road meeting from raised perspective; no ambiguous child route.
roundabout|roundabout|place|3|land|journey-places|A road junction where vehicles travel around a central area.|Vehicles move around the roundabout.|Whole central island and connected roads; direction requires locale review before arrows.
bridge|bridge|place|1|everyday|journey-places|A structure that carries a route over a gap, water or another route.|The train crosses the bridge.|Rail bridge across river with full supports and approach tracks.
tunnel|tunnel|place|2|land|journey-places|A passage through or under something, such as a hill.|The train goes through a tunnel.|Whole entrance and train with continuation inset; no dark featureless hole alone.
railway|railway|place|2|land|rail|A route with tracks for trains.|The train follows the railway tracks.|Complete track section with sleepers and train; people kept behind barrier.
station|train station|place|1|land|rail|A place where trains stop for passengers to get on or off.|We wait for our train at the station.|Station building, stopped train and platform clearly related.
platform|platform|place|2|safety|rail|The raised area beside a railway track where passengers wait.|We wait back from the edge of the platform.|Adult and child behind marked safe line, train stationary; no edge play.
bus-stop|bus stop|place|1|everyday|passengers|A marked place where buses stop for passengers.|We wait at the bus stop.|Bus-stop pole or shelter with waiting people back from road.
airport|airport|place|2|air|journey-places|A place where aircraft take off and land and passengers begin or end flights.|The family arrives at the airport.|Terminal, apron and runway context; no public access onto runway.
runway|runway|place|2|air|journey-places|A long prepared strip where aircraft take off or land.|The airplane lands on the runway.|Whole runway and aircraft with clear relationship; no road traffic.
harbour|harbour|place|2|water|journey-places|A sheltered area of water where boats and ships can stay.|The boats rest in the harbour.|Sheltered water, harbour entrance and several moored vessels; distinguish from single dock.
dock|dock|place|3|water|journey-places|A place beside the water where boats can stop for people or goods.|The ferry stops beside the dock.|Ferry secured beside boarding structure with barrier and adult supervision.
canal|canal|place|3|water|journey-places|A waterway made or changed by people for boats or water to pass along.|The boat travels along the canal.|Long narrow managed waterway and boat; walking path separated from water edge.
car-park|car park|place|1|everyday|journey-places|An area where vehicles can be parked.|The driver parks in a marked car-park space.|Parked cars and clear walking route with adult and child together.
garage|garage|place|2|helping|work|A building where vehicles are kept or repaired.|The mechanic works in a vehicle garage.|Repair garage with stationary vehicle and safe work area; not a petrol station.
fuel-station|fuel station|place|2|land|journey-places|A place where vehicles can receive fuel.|The driver stops at the fuel station.|Adult at pump, vehicle stationary, children safely inside; no child refuelling action.
passenger|passenger|person|1|everyday|passengers|A person travelling in a vehicle who is not operating it.|The passenger sits in a bus seat.|Passenger seated safely in a bus; diverse people without uniform-based guessing.
driver|driver|person|1|land|people-at-work|A person who controls a road vehicle.|The driver steers the bus.|Adult driving position in complete bus context; stationary teaching scene.
cyclist|cyclist|person|1|land|human-powered|A person riding a bicycle.|The cyclist pedals along the path.|Whole bicycle and rider with fitted helmet; role visible through action.
pedestrian|pedestrian|person|2|safety|safe-journeys|A person travelling on foot.|The pedestrians walk along the pavement.|People walking on pavement separated from vehicles; include mobility-aid user in wider scene.
pilot|pilot|person|2|air|people-at-work|A person trained to control an aircraft.|The pilot controls the airplane.|Pilot in cockpit with aircraft context; role not inferred only from costume.
boat-captain|boat captain|person|3|water|people-at-work|The person in charge of a boat or ship.|The boat captain guides the ferry.|Adult at ferry controls and whole-vessel context; varied gender and ethnicity.
train-conductor|train conductor|person|3|land|people-at-work|A railway worker who helps passengers and checks tickets on some trains.|The train conductor checks a passenger's ticket.|Worker checking ticket in carriage; distinguish conductor from train driver and state some trains.
mechanic|mechanic|person|2|helping|people-at-work|A person who maintains or repairs machines such as vehicles.|The mechanic repairs the bicycle.|Mechanic at a safe repair stand with bicycle; no child under raised vehicle.
delivery-worker|delivery worker|person|2|helping|people-at-work|A person who brings parcels or other goods to people.|The delivery worker carries a parcel to the door.|Worker and parcel beside parked vehicle; safe pavement route.
firefighter|firefighter|person|2|helping|emergency|A person trained to help put out fires and rescue people.|The firefighter takes equipment from the fire engine.|Firefighter at parked fire engine in calm scene; no distress or unsafe child imitation.
ride|ride|action|1|everyday|journey-actions|To travel on or in a vehicle or on an animal.|We ride on the bus to school.|Two-frame seated bus journey; do not imply passenger controls vehicle.
drive|drive|action|1|land|journey-actions|To control a moving road vehicle.|The adult drives the car.|Adult at controls plus moving vehicle context; no child driving real car.
balance|balance|action|2|land|journey-actions|To keep steady without falling over.|The rider balances on the bicycle.|Supervised rider with helmet in safe practice area; sequence conveys steadiness.
steer|steer|action|2|land|journey-actions|To control the direction a vehicle moves.|The driver steers around the bend.|Paired control and route view with a clear safe change of direction.
stop|stop|action|1|safety|journey-actions|To finish moving and stay still.|The bus stops at the bus stop.|Matched moving and fully stopped bus sequence; not only a red symbol.
start|start|action|1|everyday|journey-actions|To begin doing something, such as moving.|The train starts to move from the station.|Two-frame stationary-to-moving train; passengers behind safe line.
turn|turn|action|1|land|journey-actions|To change the direction you are facing or moving.|The car turns into another road.|Two-frame vehicle on clear bend; arrows meaningful and locale reviewed.
reverse|reverse|action|2|safety|journey-actions|To move backwards.|The adult checks carefully before reversing the car.|Distant safely separated observer, empty reversing area, sequential position cues.
park|park|action|1|everyday|journey-actions|To stop and leave a vehicle in a suitable place.|The driver parks in a marked space.|Sequence ends fully inside marked space, engine-off rest implied without extra vocabulary count.
board|board|action|2|everyday|journey-actions|To get onto a vehicle for a journey.|Passengers board the bus when it is safely stopped.|Adult-supervised boarding of stationary bus from pavement; clear before and after.
get-off|get off|action|1|everyday|journey-actions|To leave a vehicle at the end of a ride or at a stop.|We get off the bus with an adult.|Stationary bus and safe pavement exit; no road-side stepping.
load|load|action|2|helping|journey-actions|To put things into or onto a vehicle for carrying.|The worker loads boxes into the van.|Worker moves manageable box into parked van; no unsafe lifting.
unload|unload|action|2|helping|journey-actions|To take carried things off or out of a vehicle.|The worker unloads parcels from the van.|Matched load comparison with clear parcel moving out, vehicle stationary.
take-off|take off|action|2|air|journey-actions|To leave the ground and begin flying.|The airplane takes off from the runway.|Three positions from runway to clear air; consistent aircraft and no crowd nearby.
land|land|action|2|air|journey-actions|To return to the ground or another landing surface from flight.|The helicopter lands on its landing pad.|Three-position descent to safe pad; opposite of take-off is visually clear.
float|float|action|2|water|journey-actions|To stay supported on the surface of water or another liquid.|The boat floats on the water.|Waterline cutaway and complete boat; not a sinking comparison with people in danger.
helmet|helmet|safety|1|safety|safe-journeys|A strong covering worn to help protect the head.|An adult checks the rider's helmet fits.|Fitted cycle helmet on rider plus complete helmet; straps visible without claiming zero injury risk.
life-jacket|life jacket|safety|1|safety|safe-journeys|Equipment worn to help keep a person afloat in water.|An adult checks the child's life jacket before the boat trip.|Suitable child lifejacket correctly fastened with adult check; RNLI reference and expert review before art acceptance.
child-car-seat|child car seat|safety|2|safety|safe-journeys|A seat or restraint made to help protect a child travelling in a car.|A trusted adult checks the child's car seat.|Suitable fitted restraint in parked car with adult checking; no universal age or orientation instruction.
traffic-light|traffic light|safety|1|safety|safe-journeys|A set of coloured lights that gives signals to road users.|The driver stops when the traffic light shows red.|Whole mounted vehicle signal with red lit; separate from pedestrian signal and locale review.
stop-sign|stop sign|safety|2|safety|safe-journeys|A road sign telling drivers to stop.|The driver stops at the stop sign.|Complete octagonal sign and stationary vehicle in appropriate road context; no invented road rule.
crossing-signal|crossing signal|safety|2|safety|safe-journeys|A signal that helps people know when to wait or cross at a crossing.|We check the crossing signal with an adult.|Matched waiting and crossing indications; never imply signal alone removes need to check traffic.
reflector|reflector|safety|3|safety|safe-journeys|A surface that sends light back towards its source.|The bicycle reflector shines in the car's lights.|Same bicycle in light and dark with headlights; distinguish passive reflection from a lamp.
reflective-vest|reflective vest|safety|2|safety|safe-journeys|A vest with material that reflects light to help someone be seen.|The worker's reflective vest catches the light.|Whole vest and paired headlight scene; no implication of guaranteed visibility.
bicycle-bell|bicycle bell|safety|1|safety|safe-journeys|A small bell used by a cyclist to make a warning sound.|The cyclist rings the bicycle bell.|Bell mounted on handlebars; no implication ringing gives priority over pedestrians.
barrier|barrier|safety|2|safety|safe-journeys|Something placed to block a route or keep people away from danger.|We wait behind the closed barrier.|Rail crossing barrier with people safely behind it; never depict walking around it.
handrail|handrail|safety|1|safety|safe-journeys|A rail that people can hold for support.|The passenger holds the handrail on the steps.|Stationary bus boarding steps and adult hand contact; children supervised.
speed-limit|speed limit|safety|3|safety|safe-journeys|The highest speed allowed on a particular route.|The driver checks the speed-limit sign.|Driver-context sign, no child speed-choice quiz; number and units need locale review.
visibility|visibility|safety|3|safety|safe-journeys|How clearly people or things can be seen.|Fog can reduce a driver's visibility.|Matched clear and foggy route from same viewpoint; no independent picture-identification test.
safe-distance|safe distance|safety|3|safety|safe-journeys|Enough space to keep away from a possible danger.|We stay well back from moving vehicles with a trusted adult.|Adult and child behind safe boundary with vehicle far away; no invented universal metre value.
